{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788305383,"nonce":"91+hbpnmUB5HnN9JZdOMf51yDuE=","message":"FIXED BY SORTING THE ROWS, WHICH IS THE PREFERENCE THIS ISSUE STATES. Branch `fix/s21-suite-failures`, commit `2325285`, on top of `939f92e`. **Not run against a bench**; builds clean in Release, no assembly on the branch.\n\n## The fix\n\n`WorkCoverage.Section` now makes TWO passes over the same natural-priority-desc list from `EssentialTypes()`: every under-covered row first, then every covered row. So the string it has always published is true, and both of its clauses are — the order within each group is unchanged. The covered pass is the only one the cap can eat, which is what \"the cap only ever drops rows that are fine\" already promised. `fine` counts covered rows directly instead of `list.Count - under.Count`, which only ever worked because the two groups were interleaved in one pass.\n\nSorting rather than rewording, for the reasons the issue gives and which hold up: under-first is the more useful order for the block's actual reader, the cap already never drops an under-covered row, and a caller that walks `rows` now gets the right answer without consulting `under` at all.\n\n## The suite asserts the AGREEMENT, not either ordering\n\nThis is the part the acceptance bullet is really about, so it is worth being precise. `order_claim()` reads what `work_coverage.order` SAYS — \"under-first…\", or a string naming the inline order — and `order_agrees()` asks whether `rows` does that:\n\n- **3.7a** — the string makes a claim the suite can check. A reword to something vague fails HERE rather than sliding past, which is what makes the whole check worth anything.\n- **3.7b** — the rows are in the order the string claims. This is the assertion; it survives whichever of the issue's two fixes was chosen.\n- **3.7c** — `under` still lists exactly the under-covered rows, in the order `rows` puts them.\n- **9.3d–9.3h** — the comparator itself is proved to fail, the way 9.1–9.3 already prove `shape()`, `eq()` and `keys_exactly()`: it passes under-first data with the under-first string, FAILS the exact shape this issue was filed for (same string, under row at index 1), PASSES that same data once the string is reworded to describe it, and REFUSES a string it cannot check.\n\n## The TWO UNDER-COVERED TYPES bullet: not stageable, and here is why\n\nEvery floor but Doctor's is on **CAPABILITY** — `WorkCoverage.FloorFor` returns `1` with `capabilityFloor: true` for every `requireCapableColonist` type, and `Row.Have` is then `Capable.Count`, so `Under` means `!WorkTypeIsDisabled` is false for *every colonist on the map*. That is backstory- and trait-driven and **no shipped `dev:` verb sets it**; `dev:damage {mode:\"manipulation\"}` deliberately does not (check 4.4d asserts that losing hands is not `WorkTypeIsDisabled`). Doctor is the only availability floor and therefore the only type a fixture can push under. A second under-covered type would need a modded work type or a hand-built pawn set.\n\n**Two substitutes, both of which discriminate exactly what the bullet wants:**\n\n1. **5.6a–5.6c, live, from two reads of ONE type.** Phase 3's digest has Doctor UNDER; phase 5's post-repair digest has it COVERED. 5.6a establishes that its natural slot is not the front of the list (Doctor 1300 behind Firefighter 1400 — this issue's own evidence); 5.6b that it was at index **0** when under. So the row was moved past a covered row of higher natural priority *because* it was under-covered, which is precisely the coincidence a second under row would have ruled out. 5.6c then checks the \"then natural-priority-desc\" half without the suite ever needing to know a natural priority: strip the under rows out of the first read and the remainder must hold the same relative order as the read where nothing was under.\n2. **9.3h, offline** — `order_agrees()` with TWO under rows, in both the passing and the failing order.\n\n3.7d prints the constraint on every run rather than leaving the gap to look like laziness.\n\n## What was kept rather than rewritten\n\n**9.6f still asserts the DEFECT** against `accept/runs/s21-20260901/03-digest.json`. That envelope was banked from `1.0.0+52606d1`, before the fix, and it is the evidence this issue was filed from — re-banking it would destroy the only record of what was wrong. It now says so in its own check text.\n\nFINDING 3.7 is **promoted to a check**. It was a finding while it was a live defect, deliberately (a suite that goes permanently red over a metadata string teaches the next session to ignore its own colour); that argument stops applying the moment the defect is repaired, and a finding that outlives its fix is the suite declining to protect its own repair.\n\n## Re-run\n\nFull sweep of `./accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.py` on a fresh `--quicktest` map, **after a Release rebuild** (this is a source change). Expect 3.7a/3.7b/3.7c PASS with Doctor at index 0; 3.7d as a NOTE naming the one under type; 5.6a PASS with Doctor at index ≥ 1 in the covered read; 5.6b/5.6c PASS. 9.6f keeps passing offline against the banked pre-fix envelope, and `--selftest` is 75 checks, exit 0.","files":null}]}